The First Nations Financial Management Board (FMB) is an independent agency that assists First Nations in making progress towards financial strength and self-sufficiency. You can learn more about the FMB by visiting their website at: www.fnmfmb.com.

The FMB’s mandate is to:

strengthen First Nations’ financial management and reporting capacity;

build First Nations’ capacity to participate in the Canadian financial marketplace;

advise and assist First Nations in strengthening their financial management systems;

support First Nations in financial planning and in building and maintaining financial management systems;

encourage First Nations to participate in and contribute to the Canadian financial marketplace;

provide financial management and reporting training to First Nations;

develop financial management and reporting standards and best practices; and

encourage First Nations to adopt best practices in financial management and reporting.

The FMB offers three types of certificates:

A certificate of compliance with the standards and best practices in financial management and reporting. This certificate is valid for five years. A certificate of compliance with the standards and best practices in financial management and reporting, with a report on the results of a risk assessment. This certificate is valid for three years. A certificate of compliance with the standards and best practices in financial management and reporting, with a report on the results of a risk assessment and a recommendation to the First Nations Financial Management Act (FNFMA) Board of Directors. This certificate is valid for two years.

To obtain a certificate, First Nations must meet the FMB’s standards and best practices in financial management and reporting. These standards and best practices are outlined in the FMB’s Financial Management and Reporting Standards and Best Practices. First Nations must also meet the requirements of the FNFMA.

The FMB’s certificates are not a guarantee of financial health or a guarantee of compliance with the FNFMA. However, the certificates do indicate that the First Nation has met the FMB’s standards and best practices in financial management and reporting. This can help to build trust and confidence in the First Nation’s financial management system.

